    "deepseek": {
      "assessment": "# Audit Review: HashOddEven (BSC, 0xd09A\u20269999)\n\n**Scope note:** This is a static review of the supplied source excerpt and scanner output. The excerpt is truncated in places (e.g., `_payVIP` internals are shown, but I cannot confirm the full file matches the deployed bytecode). Treat all findings as *candidate* issues requiring local validation. I will not provide exploit transaction sequences.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Scanner triage \u2014 what is real vs. noise\n\n| Scanner finding | Verdict | Reasoning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `weak-prng` on `placeBet` / `bettingOpen` | **False positive (mislabeled)** | `block.timestamp % ROUND_SECONDS` is a *timing gate*, not randomness. It is not used to derive a winner. The actual outcome comes from `commitResult` (owner-controlled). No PRNG vulnerability here. |\n| `reentrancy-balance` in `placeBet` | **Low / likely benign** | `placeBet` is `nonReentrant`. The \"stale balance\" concern is about a malicious/rebasing token changing `balanceOf` during `transferFrom`. With a standard ERC20 this is not exploitable. Worth noting as a token-assumption risk (see \u00a73). |\n| `reentrancy-benign` in `placeBet` | **Benign** | State writes after the external call are protected by `nonReentrant`; no cross-function reentry path into `placeBet`/`settleBets`. |\n| `incorrect-equality` (\u00d74) | **False positives** | `amount == 0` guards and `amount % 10 == 0` in `visibleDigit` are intentional logic, not dangerous strict-equality bugs. |\n| `uninitialized-local` (`payout`, `reward`, `inviterPaid`) | **False positives** | Solidity 0.8 defaults locals to zero; they are assigned before use on all paths. |\n| `calls-loop` in `settleBets` | **Low** | Owner-only, `nonReentrant`, and each `_transferERC20` checks return. A reverting token would DoS settlement of that batch \u2014 operational risk, not theft. |\n\n**Net:** The scanner's \"High\" items are largely mislabeled. The *material* risks are in the business logic, not the flagged patterns.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Validated / high-confidence concerns (from source logic)\n\n### 2.1 Owner fully controls outcomes \u2014 centralization / rug vector (High)\n`commitResult` is `onlyOwner` and accepts any `result` (0/1) after the betting window. There is no commit-reveal, no oracle, no randomness. The owner can:\n- Observe all pending bets for a round, then choose the result that maximizes losses for players (or wins for whitelisted addresses).\n- Combined with `setWhitelist` (marketing) and `whitelist` forcing `won = true` in `_settle`, whitelisted accounts are **guaranteed winners** at 1.9\u00d7 payout.\n\n**Impact:** Direct, repeatable value extraction from the pool by privileged parties. This is the single most significant issue. It is a design/trust assumption, not a code bug, but it should be disclosed prominently.\n\n### 2.2 `withdrawToken` / `withdrawBNB` ignore `lockedLiability` (High)\n```solidity\nfunction withdrawToken(address erc20, address to) external onlyMarketing nonReentrant {\n    uint256 amount = IERC20(erc20).balanceOf(address(this));\n    _transferERC20(erc20, to, amount);   // no lockedLiability check\n}\nfunction withdrawBNB(address payable to) external onlyMarketing nonReentrant {\n    _sendBNB(to, address(this).balance); // no liability check\n}\n```\nThe contract carefully enforces `lockedLiability` on `placeBet`, but the marketing wallet can drain the **entire** token balance (including funds backing pending bets) at any time. The comment even admits \"may affect pending payouts.\"\n\n**Impact:** Pending winners can be left unpaid; `_settle` will revert on `_safeTransfer` if balance is insufficient, bricking settlement. This is a privileged rug/insolvency vector.\n\n### 2.3 `_settle` accounting: `lockedLiability` underflow / mismatch (Medium-High)\n`placeBet` adds `_maxLiability(received)` to `lockedLiability`. `_settle` subtracts `_maxLiability(amount)` where `amount = b.amount`. These match **only if** `b.amount` equals the `received` recorded at bet time \u2014 which it does. So the arithmetic is consistent *per bet*.\n\nHowever:\n- `_maxLiability` includes `MAX_REWARD_BPS` (4.5%) for the inviter reward, but the actual reward paid is `rewardBps(vipLevel[inviter])` (2%\u20134.5%). The reserve is conservative \u2014 fine.\n- **But** if `withdrawToken` drains the pool (\u00a72.2), `lockedLiability` remains high while balance is low; subsequent `placeBet` calls revert `InsufficientPool`, and `_settle` reverts on transfer. No underflow, but a **liveness/insolvency** failure.\n\n### 2.4 `visibleDigit` / guess derivation is manipulable by the player (Medium)\nThe \"guess\" is derived from the *received amount*:\n```solidity\nuint8 guess = uint8(visibleDigit(received) % 2);\n```\n`visibleDigit` strips trailing zeros up to `ONE`. A player can choose `amount` to force either parity (e.g., `1e18` \u2192 digit 1 \u2192 odd; `2e18` \u2192 digit 2 \u2192 even). This is by design (\"\u5c3e\u6570\u5947\u6570\u731c\u5355\"), but it means the player **always knows their own guess** and can pick it freely \u2014 combined with owner-controlled results (\u00a72.1), the house has full information advantage. Not a bug per se, but the \"guess\" is not a commitment to anything the player can't trivially set.\n\n### 2.5 `_payVIP` BNB distribution \u2014 inviter call with 30k gas (Low-Medium)\n```solidity\n(bool ok,) = payable(inviter).call{value: inviterPart, gas: 30_000}(\"\");\nif (ok) inviterPaid = inviterPart;\nelse marketingPart += inviterPart;\n```\n- Failure is handled (falls back to marketing) \u2014 good.\n- But `_sendBNB(owner, ownerPart)` and `_sendBNB(marketing, marketingPart)` **revert on failure**. If `owner` or `marketing` is a contract that rejects BNB, all VIP payments revert. Operational risk, not theft.\n- Rounding: `ownerPart + marketingPart + inviterPart` may be \u2264 `value` due to integer division; dust remains in contract. Minor.\n\n### 2.6 `grantVIP` can be used to inflate inviter rewards (Low-Medium)\n`grantVIP` (marketing-only) sets `vipPaid[user]` to the threshold and `vipLevel`. Since `_settle` pays the **inviter** based on `vipLevel[inviter]`, marketing can grant VIP to colluding inviters to boost their cut (up to 4.5% vs 2%). This is a privileged parameter, but it directly increases payouts from the pool. Trust assumption.\n\n### 2.7 `bindInviter` cycle check is bounded to 64 hops (Low)\nThe loop `for (i < 64 && cur != 0)` stops after 64 iterations. A chain longer than 64 could allow a cycle to be created, though `inviters[user] != 0` prevents rebinding. Low practical impact given owner-only binding, but the bound is a correctness assumption worth noting.\n\n### 2.8 `setBetLimits` allows `maxBet` unbounded (Low)\n`maxBet_ < minBet_` is checked, but no upper cap. Owner can set `maxBet` to a huge value; `_maxLiability` could overflow? Solidity ",
